<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  ⚠️ STILL NOT WIRED UP. CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS is deliberately NOT set in
	  project.pbxproj, because setting it fails the build.

	  DONE (2026-08-24): the App ID `com.templetongroup.radiant` is now
	  registered explicitly in the developer portal with Increased Memory Limit
	  enabled. It did not exist before — Radiant had been signing against the
	  XC Wildcard App ID (`*`), and a WILDCARD APP ID CANNOT CARRY CAPABILITIES,
	  which is why there was no checkbox to find.

	  REMAINING, AND HUMAN-ONLY: Xcode has no Apple ID signed in.
	      $ defaults read com.apple.dt.Xcode DVTDeveloperAccountManagerAppleIDLists
	      { "IDE.Identifiers.Prod" = ( ); }
	  With no account, -allowProvisioningUpdates cannot generate a profile for
	  the new App ID, so the build keeps falling back to the wildcard and fails:
	      No Accounts: Add a new account in Accounts settings.
	      Provisioning profile "iOS Team Provisioning Profile: *" doesn't
	      include the Increased Memory Limit capability.
	  Signing in requires a password, which no agent may type.

	  ONCE TONY SIGNS IN (Xcode → Settings → Accounts → +, then pick the team
	  and Download Manual Profiles), add
	      CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS = App/App.entitlements;
	  to BOTH build configurations in project.pbxproj and rebuild.

	  ⚠️ THIS ENTITLEMENT IS WHAT MAKES THE LARGER MODELS POSSIBLE AT ALL.

	  iOS gives an ordinary app only a fraction of the device's RAM before
	  jetsam kills it — on a 12 GB iPhone that can be under half. A 4 GB model
	  needs about 5 GB resident, so without this the top of the catalogue is
	  permanently "won't run" on hardware that is physically capable of it.

	  With it, iOS raises this process's limit toward the device maximum. It
	  changes what os_proc_available_memory() reports, which is the number the
	  fit labels are computed from — so removing it does not just lose
	  capability, it silently re-labels most of the catalogue.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
